ecting none will make all of them available.','','none'),(7941,'You selected the \"@operator\" operator as the default value but is not included in the list of limited operators.','','none'),(7942,'Provides an interface to create workflows with transitions between different states (for example publication or user status) provided by other modules.','','11.4.9'),(7943,'Indicates the workspace that this revision belongs to.','','none'),(7944,'Switch to Live','','none'),(7945,'Would you like to switch to the live version of the site?','','none'),(7946,'Switch to the live version of the site.','','none'),(7947,'You are now viewing the live version of the site.','','none'),(7948,'Media Library thumbnail (220×220)','','none'),(7949,'Delete {{ name }}','','none'),(7950,'Viewing help topics','','none'),(7951,'The top-level help topics are listed on the main <a href=\":help_page\">Help page</a>. Links to other topics, including non-top-level help topics, can be found under the \"Related\" heading when viewing a topic page.','','none'),(7952,'Providing help topics','','none'),(7953,'Translating help topics','','none'),(7954,'The title and body text of help topics provided by contributed modules and themes are translatable using the <a href=\":locale_help\">Interface Translation module</a>. Topics provided by custom modules and themes are also translatable if they have been viewed at least once in a non-English language, which triggers putting their translatable text into the translation database.','','none'),(7955,'Configuring help search','','none'),(7956,'See the <a href=\":help_page\">Help page</a> for more topics.','','none'),(7957,'Help search','','none'),(7958,'Configure the settings of a block that was previously placed in a region of a theme.','','none'),(7959,'Click the name of the theme that contains the block.','','none'),(7960,'Optionally, click <em>Demonstrate block regions</em> to see the regions of the theme.','','none'),(7961,'If you only want to change the region where a block is located, or the ordering of blocks within a region, drag blocks to their desired positions and click <em>Save blocks</em>.','','none'),(7962,'If you want to change additional settings, find the region where the block you want to update is currently located, and click <em>Configure</em> in the line of the block description.','','none'),(7963,'Edit the block\'s settings. The available settings vary depending on the module that provides the block, but for all blocks you can change:','','none'),(7964,'<em>Block title</em>: The heading for the block on your site -- for some blocks, you will need to check the <em>Override title</em> checkbox in order to enter a title','','none'),(7965,'<em>Display title</em>: Check the box if you want the title displayed','','none'),(7966,'<em>Visibility</em>: Add conditions for when the block should be displayed','','none'),(7967,'<em>Region</em>: Change the theme region the block is displayed in','','none'),(7968,'Click <em>Save block</em>.','','none'),(7969,'What are blocks?','','none'),(7970,'Blocks are boxes of content rendered into an area, or region, of a web page of your site. Blocks are placed and configured specifically for each theme.','','none'),(7971,'What is the block description?','','none'),(7972,'The block description is an identification name for a block, which is shown in the administrative interface. It is not displayed on the site.','','none'),(7973,'What is the block title?','','none'),(7974,'The block title is the heading that is optionally shown to site visitors when the block is placed in a region.','','none'),(7975,'Place a block into a theme\'s region.','','none'),(7976,'Click the name of the theme that you want to place the block in.','','none'),(7977,'Find the region where you want the block, and click <em>Place block</em> in that region. A modal dialog will pop up.','','none'),(7978,'Find the block you want to place and click <em>Place block</em>. A <em>Configure block</em> modal dialog will pop up.','','none'),(7979,'Enter a description of your block (to be shown to administrators) and the body text for your block.','','none'),(7980,'Enter a label for this block type (shown in the administrative interface). Optionally, edit the automatically-generated machine name or the description.','','none'),(7981,'Click <em>Save</em>. You will be returned to the <em>Block types</em> page.','','none'),(7982,'Click <em>Manage fields</em> in the row of your new block type, and add the desired fields to your block type.','','none'),(7983,'Optionally, click <em>Manage form display</em> or <em>Manage display</em> to change the editing form or field display for your block type.','','none'),(7984,'Use contextual links to access administrative tasks without navigating the administrative menu.','','none'),(7985,'What are contextual links?','','none'),(7986,'<em>Contextual links</em> give users with the <em>Use contextual links</em> permission quick access to administrative tasks related to areas of non-administrative pages. For example, if a page on your site displays a block, the block would have a contextual link that would allow users with permission to configure the block. If the block contains a menu or a view, it would also have a contextual link for editing the menu links or the view. Clicking a contextual link takes you to the related administrative page directly, without needing to navigate through the administrative menu system.','','none'),(7987,'Make sure that the core Contextual Links module is installed, and that you have a role with the <em>Use contextual links</em> permission. Optionally, make sure that a toolbar module is installed (either the core Toolbar module or a contributed module replacement).','','none'),(7988,'Visit a non-administrative page on your site, such as the home page.','','none'),(7989,'Locate a block or another area on the page that you want to edit or configure.','','none'),(7990,'Make the contextual links button visible by hovering your mouse over that area in the page. In most themes, this button looks like a pencil and is placed in the upper right corner of the page area (upper left for right-to-left languages), and hovering will also temporarily outline the affected area. Alternatively, click the contextual links toggle button on the right end of the toolbar (left end for right-to-left languages), which will make all contextual link buttons on the page visible until it is clicked again.','','none'),(7991,'While the contextual links button for the area of interest is visible, click the button to display the list of links for that area. Click a link in the list to visit the corresponding administrative page.','','none'),(7992,'Complete your administrative task and save your settings, or cancel the action. You should be returned to the page you started from.','','none'),(7993,'What are security updates?','','none'),(7994,'Any software occasionally has bugs, and sometimes these bugs have security implications. When security bugs are fixed in the core software, modules, or themes that your site uses, they are released in a <em>security update</em>. You will need to apply security updates in order to keep your site secure.','','none'),(7995,'Security tasks','','none'),(7996,'Keeping track of updates, updating the core software, and updating contributed modules and/or themes are all part of keeping your site secure. See the related topics listed below for specific tasks.','','none'),(7997,'Overview of accessibility','','none'),(7998,'The default drag-and-drop user interface for ordering tables in the administrative interface presents a challenge for some users, including keyboard-only users and users of screen readers and other assistive technology. The drag-and-drop interface can be disabled in a table by clicking a link labeled <em>Show row weights</em> above the table. The replacement interface allows users to order the table by choosing numerical weights (with increasing numbers) instead of dragging table rows.','','none'),(7999,'Administrative interface overview','','none'),(8000,'A menu system, which you can navigate to find pages for administrative tasks. The core Toolbar module displays this menu on the top or left side of the page (right side in right-to-left languages). There are also contributed module replacements for the core Toolbar module, with additional features, such as the <a href=\"https://www.drupal.org/project/admin_toolbar\">Admin Toolbar module</a>.','','none'),(8001,'If you install the core Contextual Links module, non-administrative pages will contain links leading to related administrative tasks.','','none'),(8002,'The core Help module displays help topics, and provides a Help block that can be placed on administrative pages to provide an overview of their functionality.','','none'),(8003,'See the related topics listed below for specific tasks.','','none'),(8004,'Set up your site so that users can search for help.','','none'),(8005,'In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Search and metadata</em> &gt; <em>Search pages</em>.','','none'),(8006,'Verify that a Help search page is listed in the <em>Search pages</em> section. If not, add a new page of type <em>Help</em>.','','none'),(8007,'Create, view, and use a set of shortcuts to access administrative pages.','','none'),(8008,'What are shortcuts?','','none'),(8009,'Navigate to an administrative page that you want in your shortcut list.','','none'),(8010,'Repeat until all the desired links have been added to your shortcut set.','','none'),(8011,'Click <em>Shortcuts</em> in the toolbar to display your shortcuts, and verify that the list is complete.','','none'),(8012,'Optionally, click <em>Edit shortcuts</em> at the right end of the shortcut list (left end in right-to-left languages), to remove links or change their order.','','none'),(8013,'Click any link in the shortcut bar to go directly to the administrative page.','','none'),(8014,'Configure the basic settings of your site, including the site name, slogan, main email address, default time zone, default country, and the date formats to use.','','none'),(8015,'Click <em>Save configuration</em>. You should see a message indicating that the settings were saved.','','none'),(8016,'Select the default country and default time zone for your site.','','none'),(8017,'Look at the <em>Patterns</em> for the Default long, medium, and short date formats. If any of them does not match the date format you want to use on your site, click <em>Edit</em> in that row to edit the format.','','none'),(8018,'Adjust the <em>Format string</em> until the <em>Displayed</em> format matches what you want. (Date format strings are composed of PHP date format codes.)','','none'),(8019,'Click <em>Save format</em>. You should see a message indicating that the format was saved.','','none'),(8020,'Repeat the previous three steps for any other date formats that need to be changed.','','none'),(8021,'Set up your site to respond appropriately to site errors, including 403 and 404 page responses.','','none'),(8022,'What are 403 and 404 responses?','','none'),(8023,'When a user visits a web page, the web server sends a response code in addition to the page content. A normal, non-error response has code 200. If the page does not exist on the site, the response code is 404. If the page exists, but the user is not authorized to visit the page, the response code is 403. The core software provides default responses for both 403 and 404 codes, but if you prefer, you can create your own pages for each.','','none'),(8024,'What other errors can occur?','','none'),(8025,'Under some situations, your site can generate error messages. These can be due to user errors (such as entering invalid values in a form, or incorrect configuration), PHP runtime errors, or software bugs. Some errors may result in a <em>white screen of death</em> (a totally blank web page response); less drastic errors will generate error messages. You can configure what happens when an error message is generated.','','none'),(8026,'If desired, create pages to use for 403 and 404 responses. Note the URLs for these pages.','','none'),(8027,'For a production site, select <em>None</em> under <em>Error messages to display</em>. For a site that is in development, select one of the other options, so that you are more aware of the errors the site is generating.','','none'),(8028,'Configure settings related to how user accounts are created and deleted.','','none'),(8029,'What are the settings related to user account creation and deletion?','','none'),(8030,'You can make it possible for new users to register themselves for accounts, with or without email verification or administrative approval. Or, you can make it so only administrators with <em>Administer users</em> permission can register new users.','','none'),(8031,'You can configure what happens to content that a user created, if their account is <em>canceled</em> (deleted).','','none'),(8032,'You can edit the email messages that are sent to users when their accounts are pending, approved, created, blocked, or canceled, or when they request a password reset.','','none'),(8033,'What are variables in email message text?','','none'),(8034,'<em>Variables</em> are short text strings, enclosed in square brackets [], that you can insert into configured email message text. When an individual message is generated, data from your site is substituted for the variables. Some commonly-used variables are:','','none'),(8035,'[site:login-url]: The URL where users can log in to your site.','','none'),(8036,'[user:display-name]: The user\'s displayed name.','','none'),(8037,'[user:mail]: The user\'s email alias.','','none'),(8038,'[user:one-time-login-url]: An expiring URL that a user can use to log in once, if they need to reset their password.','','none'),(8039,'Select the method you want to use for creating user accounts, and check or uncheck the box that requires email verification, to match the settings you want for your site.','','none'),(8040,'Select the desired option for what happens to content that a user created if their account is canceled.','','none'),(8041,'Optionally, edit the text of email messages related to user accounts.','','none'),(8042,'Verify that the other settings are correct.','','none'),(8043,'Topics can be provided by modules or themes. Top-level help topics on your site:','','none'),(8044,'Both the table-style and grid-style interfaces are regular views and can be customized via the <a href=\":views-ui\">Views UI</a>, including sorting and filtering. This is the case for both the administration page and the modal dialog.','','none'),(8045,'In the grid-style interface, the fields that are displayed (including which image style is used for images) can be customized by configuring the \"Media library\" view mode for each of your <a href=\":media-types\">media types</a>. The thumbnail images in the grid-style interface can be customized by configuring the \"Media Library thumbnail (220×220)\" image style.','','none'),(8046,'When adding new media items within the modal dialog, the fields that are displayed can be customized by configuring the \"Media library\" form mode for each of your <a href=\":media-types\">media types</a>.','','none'),(8047,'Provides the API allowing to rename URLs.','','none'),(8048,'The parent workspace.','','none'),(8049,'Merge workspace','','none'),(8050,'Only top-level workspaces can be published.','','none'),(8051,'Merge into @target_label','','none'),(8052,'The %label workspace can not be deleted because it has child workspaces.','','none'),(8053,'There are no changes that can be merged from %source_label to %target_label.','','none'),(8054,'Would you like to merge the contents of the %source_label workspace into %target_label?','','none'),(8055,'Merge workspace contents.','','none'),(8056,'The contents of the %source_label workspace have been merged into %target_label